{
  "schema": "terminal-wait-whp-rf-independent-review-v1",
  "date": "2026-11-10",
  "baseline": "fe5d2d84",
  "scope": "Independent offline source and raw artifact review only; reviewer started no native probe, VM or guest. One new report; frozen sources and old reports unchanged.",
  "decision": {
    "finite_owned_guest_contrasts_verified": true,
    "automatic_rf_clear_rejected": true,
    "completed_near_ret_rf_zero_general_proof": false,
    "transparent_original_fullstate": false,
    "canonical57_accepted": false,
    "original_game_accepted": false,
    "original_game_starts": 0,
    "windows_boots": 0
  },
  "conclusion": [
    "With real DR0=307b and DR7=401, target execution #DB runs before return-page EPT denial. RET origin plus matching restored stack is insufficient provenance: the immediately preceding transfer at the observed EPT point can be the guest handler IRETD restoring RF=1.",
    "Preserving raw RF reproduces both corresponding no-return-EPT baselines. Unsafe RF clearing duplicates RET #DB (1 to 2) or introduces IRET #DB (0 to 1), despite consumer executing once. No successful constrained RF restoration arm is established.",
    "RET unsafe final CPU register values and XSAVE equal the preserve arm; only handler count differs in memory. Final register equality alone cannot establish debug-event or resource behavior equivalence."
  ],
  "primary_contract": [
    {
      "url": "https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/developer/articles/technical/intel-sdm.html",
      "revision": "093; portal updated 2026-10-09; linked volumes September 2026"
    },
    {
      "url": "https://cdrdv2.intel.com/v1/dl/getContent/671427",
      "section": "Vol 3B 20.3.1.1, PDF pages 153-154 zero-based",
      "finding": "RF suppresses instruction breakpoints. RF clears at instruction start after breakpoint/CS-limit/FP checks. IRETD/IRETQ load RF from the saved image. Instruction #DB does not itself force stacked RF=1; the handler can set it."
    },
    {
      "url": "https://cdrdv2.intel.com/v1/dl/getContent/671506",
      "section": "Vol 3C 30.3.3; 31.3.3.3",
      "finding": "Non-event-delivery EPT exits save RF=1, losing its prior bit. Event-delivery cases have different rules. EPT translation ordering is specified separately; it does not make every instruction fetch the earliest observable event."
    },
    {
      "url": "https://cdrdv2.intel.com/v1/dl/getContent/671190",
      "section": "Vol 3A Table 7-2",
      "finding": "Instruction-breakpoint faults precede instruction-fetch faults in the instruction-boundary event table. This table alone is not a blanket proof for all EPT exits during instruction execution or event delivery. Actual target-fetch order is additionally demonstrated here by guest handler memory before EPT."
    },
    {
      "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/virtualization/api/hypervisor-platform/funcs/virtualprocessorexception",
      "finding": "WHP exception exits require ExceptionExit enabled. The reviewed probe does not enable interception or write pending-event registers; #DB follows the actual guest IDT."
    },
    {
      "local_sdk": {
        "path": "/mnt/c/Program Files (x86)/Windows Kits/10/Include/10.0.26100.0/um/WinHvPlatformDefs.h",
        "bytes": 138461,
        "sha256": "91a864b6ec2d932bcc7ce17f503f72a7813759cde81be2d1ac86cba9abb61c4a"
      },
      "lines": "1001-1019; 1229-1245; 2207",
      "finding": "PendingExceptionEvent is a 16-byte delivery request including EventPending/type/vector/error code/parameter. PendingDebugException is a distinct 8-byte breakpoint/single-step mask. Neither is evidence of transparent reinjection merely from its type definition. No host exception injection is used or certified here."
    }
  ],
